# Your environment: ccic container `{{ .Cfg.Suffix }}`

You are running inside a Docker container that belongs to you alone. The human
runs their own copy of this project directly on their machine. **The two
environments are deliberately kept apart** — you never share a port, a database,
or a compiled dependency with them. Nothing you start here can collide with
anything they have running.

## Showing the human something visual

Write images to **`{{ .Cfg.Isolation.Screenshots }}/`**. That directory is on the
bind mount, so a file you write there opens immediately on their machine.
{{ if not .Cfg.Network.Publish }}
This is the *only* channel for visual feedback — no port is published, so they
cannot open a dev server you start. If you need them to see rendering, take a
screenshot and tell them the path, relative to the project root:
{{- else }}
These host ports are published, so a dev server you start on one is visible to
them:{{ range .Cfg.Network.Publish }} `{{ . }}`{{ end }}. For anything else, take
a screenshot and tell them the path, relative to the project root:
{{- end }}

```
{{ .Cfg.Isolation.Screenshots }}/checkout-form-overflow.png
```

Name files for what they show, not `screenshot-1.png`. You can read your own
screenshots back to check your work.

Two ways to produce one:

- `ccic-shot <url|file> <output.png> [width] [height]` — one command, no script.
- For anything more involved, write a script in **`/opt/ccic/browser/`** and run
  it there. That directory has Playwright resolvable, so `import { chromium }
  from 'playwright'` works. It will *not* work from a script inside this project,
  because Playwright is installed globally and ESM cannot resolve global
  packages. Keep browser scratch work out of the project.

## What is installed

- **Playwright + Chromium**, headless, with fonts and colour emoji, so rendered
  text in screenshots is real text rather than boxes. This is ccic's Playwright
  for your own visual checks — it is not the project's test dependency.
- **Node {{ .NodeMajor }}** at `/usr/bin/node`, outside mise. Claude Code and
  Playwright run on it, so a project pinning a different node version cannot
  disturb them.
{{- if .MiseTools }}
- **mise**, with this project's toolchain installed:{{ range .MiseTools }} `{{ . }}`{{ end }}.
  Inside the workspace these take precedence, so `node`, `python`, `ruby` and so
  on are the project's versions.
{{- else }}
- **mise** is available, but this project declares no `[tools]`, so you get
  ccic's node and nothing else.
{{- end }}
- `git`, `ripgrep`, `fd`, `jq`, `psql`, and a normal build toolchain
  (`build-essential`, `pkg-config`, `libvips`, `libpq-dev`, `libyaml-dev`).

Start the app yourself if you need to drive it — that is what the toolchain is for.

## Database
{{ if .Cfg.Postgres.Enabled }}
PostgreSQL {{ .Cfg.Postgres.Version }} runs in a second container on host `db`,
port 5432. `DATABASE_URL`, `PGHOST`, `PGUSER`, `PGPASSWORD` and `PGDATABASE` are
already exported, so `psql` with no arguments connects to
`{{ .Cfg.Postgres.Database }}`.

This database is reachable from this container and from nowhere else — not the
host machine, not the internet. It is **not** the human's development database,
so migrate, seed, reset and drop it freely. Schema files you change
(`schema.rb`, migrations) are on the bind mount and do reach them.
{{- else }}
No database container is configured for this project.
{{- end }}
{{- if .Cfg.Redis.Enabled }}

Redis {{ .Cfg.Redis.Version }} is on host `redis`, port 6379, via `REDIS_URL`.
{{- end }}

## Paths that are container-only

These are **yours**, not shared with the host, because native extensions built
here are Linux and the host's are macOS. Mixing them corrupts both installs.
{{ range .Cfg.Isolation.Paths }}
- `{{ . }}/`
{{- end }}

Running `npm install` and its equivalents in here is safe and does not touch the
host's copy. Bundler, uv, pip and cargo are redirected out of the workspace
entirely, via `BUNDLE_PATH`, `UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT`, `PIP_CACHE_DIR` and
`CARGO_TARGET_DIR`.

Everything *else* under `{{ .Cfg.Workspace }}` is a live bind mount of the
host's repository. Your edits are immediately real on their machine — there is
no sync step and no undo.

## Git: commit freely, you cannot push
{{ if .Cfg.Git.Identity }}
Commit as much as you like; the human's name and email are already configured.
{{- else }}
Commit as much as you like.
